            <ArticleTitle>The need for surgery in acute abdominal pain: a randomized study of abdominal computed tomography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>305-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the need for surgical treatment in patients with acute abdominal pain in a prospective randomized study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Initially 203 patients with acute abdominal pain were randomized to the routine abdominal computed tomography CT (rCT, n=118), or selective abdominal CT group (sCT, n=85) over a period of 16 months. Ninety-three of the randomized patients (45.8%) underwent the study design and were reached for follow-up at three months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Diagnostic accuracy improved significantly in the rCT group (p&lt;0.001). The surgeon's assessment of the need for surgery changed more often in the rCT group than in the sCT group (78.7% vs. 46.9%, p=0.002). The confidence to treat operatively increased significantly in the rCT vs. the sCT group (65.6% vs. 40.6%, p=0.028). The rCT was the only independent parameter for the change of the assessment of surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Routine CT allows for more confidence in decision making for the surgical treatment of patients with acute abdominal pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
